#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
Horde Studio Lite
=================
A single-file PyQt6 desktop client for the AI Horde (Stable Horde / hAIdra).

Design goals
------------
* Pure REST approach (same as the official AI Horde CLI): POST /generate/async,
  poll /generate/check/{id}, fetch /generate/status/{id}, download the R2 image.
* No Horde-specific SDK. Only PyQt6 is required; `requests` is used when
  installed, otherwise the standard-library urllib is used transparently.
  `Pillow` (optional) enables true Lanczos resampling for the tile upscale;
  without it the upscale falls back to Qt's smooth transform.
* Browser bridge (VPN tunnel, optional): every Horde API request can be routed
  through a page of your browser (https://stablehorde.net/api/ with the bundled
  Tampermonkey userscript, horde_studio_bridge.user.js). Because the request
  then travels through the browser's network stack, any VPN extension active
  in that browser applies to it. The app runs a token-protected local server
  (default 127.0.0.1:7862) the userscript connects to; see the HORDE tab.
* Local server for SillyTavern (optional): a small A1111-compatible
  /sdapi/v1 endpoint (default 127.0.0.1:7860). Paste the address into
  SillyTavern's Image Generation panel (source: "Stable Diffusion WebUI");
  generation requests are redirected to the Horde with the app's currently
  active model (Anima or Krea). Only the prompt is taken from the request -
  negative prompt and other parameters are ignored in favour of this app's
  settings. The finished image is saved into temp/ (or output/ with direct
  saving) and returned to SillyTavern as base64.
* Resilient networking: a urllib3 Retry adapter (connection-level retries with
  back-off, also for POSTs whose socket died before the request went out -
  the classic aihorde.net "SSLEOFError" stale keep-alive failure), session
  recycling on connection errors and call-level retries on top.
* Everything lives next to this file:  key.txt, settings.json, llm_settings.json,
  templates_anime.xml, templates_krea.xml, tag_groups.txt, wildcards/ (dynamicprompts
  wildcard files), temp/ (last 10 renders), output/ (kept renders), tiles/
  (tile-upscale work area). A checkbox can send finished renders straight into
  output/ instead of temp.
* Alchemy (image interrogation): an optional checkbox sends every finished render
  for an aesthetic score; the score is stored with the image metadata and shown
  on the thumbnail, the caption bar and the tooltip.
* Tile upscale (UPSCALE tab): A1111-style tiled upscale + img2img diffusion.
  The WHOLE source image is magnified exactly once (one Alchemy upscaler
  request, or pure Lanczos when the "Lanczos only" pseudo-upscaler is chosen),
  then non-neurally resized to the exact target size with Lanczos (Pillow),
  THEN split into an overlapping grid of ~square tiles (a square source
  always yields a square N\u00d7N grid; every tile as sent - overlap included -
  stays within one megapixel). Each tile is refined via img2img and merged
  back over the upscaled base with smooth feathered transitions, so a failed
  tile degrades gracefully to the neural base instead of leaving a hole.
  Every stage is a real queue task: the "Tile Upscale" run sits pinned on top
  of the queue and is composed of the inner Alchemy and img2img API requests,
  which are batched automatically up to the "simultaneous requests" limit.
  Live previews show the tile grid on the source and the growing result
  (unfilled space in maximum-saturation purple until the base lands, then the
  dimmed neural base with refined tiles blended over it); the final image is
  assembled automatically once every tile reached a terminal state.
  v1.6.2 img2img tile fixes: every tile model now sends the CLIP skip it was
  trained with (2 for all the SDXL anime / Pony / Illustrious lineages listed
  here - the Horde's default of 1 conditioned these models on a CLIP layer
  they never saw in training, which muddied and darkened the tiles); tile
  sizes sent to the Horde are always multiples of 64 (anything else makes
  the payload verification fail once the overlap is not 64-aligned); a tile
  that comes back CENSORED - the worker's black NSFW placeholder, the
  classic "every tile is black and empty" failure - is now detected via the
  per-generation `censored` flag AND a near-black frame check, treated as a
  failed tile (it keeps the neural base instead of stamping a black
  rectangle over the result) and explained in the log, with a preflight
  warning when the request options are flagged SFW/censoring.
* Wildcards: when the `dynamicprompts` package is installed, __wildcard__ files
  from the ./wildcards folder and {a|b} variants are resolved in the prompt.
  v1.7.0 adds __[Folder]__ references: the token is replaced with a RANDOM tag
  group from that folder of tag_groups.txt (works without dynamicprompts, as
  does a built-in fallback for {a|b} variants and __wildcard__ files).
* v1.7.0 GALLERY tab (right panel): a paginated, ACDSee-style browser over the
  output/ folder - asynchronous thumbnail decoding, sorting by date /
  aesthetic score / name, Anima / Krea badges, multi-select delete, copying the
  full or the unique (pre-template) prompt, and one-click dispatch to the
  viewer or the tile-upscale tab.
* v1.7.0 LLM tab: forge prompt sequences with any OpenAI-compatible endpoint.
  The API URL ends with /v1; /chat/completions and /models are appended
  automatically. Prompts are extracted from <prompt></prompt> blocks and can
  be copied or sent straight into the prompt editor. LLM settings live in
  their own llm_settings.json next to the app.
* v1.9.0 LoRAs rebuilt around one source of truth: `self._lora_stacks` is
  updated IMMEDIATELY on every edit (enable checkbox, weights, trigger,
  add / remove) - the old table-item checkbox only scheduled a 900 ms settings
  save, so a toggled row could visibly fail to stick and the caption stayed
  stale. Each row now carries a real QCheckBox, disabled rows are greyed out,
  and every change syncs the active model's stack before anything else reads
  it. The `loras` array still sits at the TOP LEVEL of the Horde payload (the
  API schema puts it next to `models`), and each generation model (Anima
  Turbo / Krea 2 Turbo) keeps its OWN stack that follows the deck selector.
  SillyTavern requests no longer clobber the active stack when they carry
  their own `loras`.
* v1.9.0 viewer + gallery: Ctrl+wheel zoom with cursor anchoring, drag-to-pan,
  zoom controls, a percentage readout, a slideshow mode and keyboard strip
  navigation. The output browser gains an INFO dialog (full metadata + the
  complete parameter block), in-place RENAME, an aesthetic-score filter, a
  dimensions / size line in the status bar, richer context menus, Ctrl+C
  (copy image) / Ctrl+P (copy prompt) shortcuts and page-aware navigation.
* v1.8.0 viewer + gallery: search box (prompt / filename), model filter
  (All / KREA / ANIMA), three tile sizes, selection-aware buttons and
  copy-image / copy-path actions.
* v1.9.0 in-app editors: the TEMPLATES and TAGS editors are the intended
  (and now comfortable) way to manage templates_*.xml / tag_groups.txt -
  add / edit / duplicate / remove / re-folder, template re-ordering with an
  {{insert}}-validation hint and a live composed preview; tag groups gain a
  search filter, move up / down re-ordering and folder colour chips. The
  files stay plain text/XML so they can still be edited outside.
* v1.9.0 twin themes, fully themeable chrome, tuned for East-Asian design
  tastes: dense orderly layout, hairline borders, tight radii, restrained
  gloss - nothing western-round, nothing historical. "Kintsugi Gold" is the
  deep lacquer black + polished gold look (Deus Ex: Human Revolution
  flavour). "Crimson Shrine" is a strict JUXTAPOSITION: PURE neutral black
  (no reddish grey, no reddish black anywhere) against ultra-saturated PURE
  red (#ff0000 family, no purple, no blend) - Babymetal / Blood-C / Kuon
  flavoured dark-oriental folklore chrome, old-school glossy bevels.
  Switch live from the tab-bar corner button, the View menu or
  Ctrl+Shift+T; no restart, no glitches, text contrast tuned for both.
* Tag groups are organised in folders (Artists, Characters, Clothes, Scenery,
  ...) and every inserted group keeps its folder colour inside the prompt until
  that passage is edited.
* Optimised for small screens (1024x768 / 800x600) with slightly oversized,
  high-contrast controls and wide (22 px) easy-to-grab scrollbars. Two
  complete dark lacquer themes - black+gold and black+crimson - with glossy
  old-school (non-flat) bevels throughout, switchable at runtime.

Install / run
-------------
    pip install PyQt6 requests
    pip install dynamicprompts      # optional: extended wildcard syntax (a built-in
                                    # fallback covers {a|b}, __file__ and __[Folder]__)
    pip install Pillow              # optional: Lanczos resampling (tile upscale)
    python horde_studio_lite.py

Put your API key in key.txt (one line). Register for free at https://aihorde.net

Browser bridge quick start
--------------------------
    1. HORDE tab \u2192 "Browser bridge (VPN)" \u2192 tick the checkbox (server starts
       on 127.0.0.1:7862 by default; a random token is generated for you).
    2. Install horde_studio_bridge.user.js in Tampermonkey (new user script,
       paste the file, save).
    3. Open https://stablehorde.net/api/ (or https://aihorde.net/api/) in the
       browser with the VPN extension active. The userscript panel appears in
       the corner; paste the token from the HORDE tab once and press CONNECT.
    4. Keep that tab open while generating. Every Horde request (including
       image downloads) now leaves through the browser - and its VPN.
"""
